AB  - Abstract Brown planthopper ( Nilaparvata lugens ) is one of the most destructive pests in tropical rice farming ecosystems. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of Beauveria bassiana local isolates as a biological control agent in an environmentally friendly rice cultivation system. The field experiment was conducted using a Randomized Block Design (RBD) with five dose treatments (0, 1106, 1107, 1108, and 1109 spores/mL) and five replications. The parameters observed included planthopper mortality levels, population density, attack intensity, and rice yield. The ANOVA and DMRT results (p B. bassiana is practical and applicable as a biological agent, supporting sustainable agriculture principles and potentially reducing reliance on synthetic pesticides in Integrated Pest Management (IPM) programs.
