AB  - The entomopathogenic fungus Beauveria bassiana is promising for biological pest control, but its field application is limited by poor environmental stress tolerance of conidia. Here, we overexpressed two transcription factor genes, VvbHLH and VvCBF, from the thermotolerant fungus Volvariella volvacea in B. bassiana. The resulting strains exhibited significantly enhanced conidial tolerance to heat, oxidative, osmotic, and UV stresses, along with increased insecticidal virulence against Galleria mellonella larvae (35% reduction in LT50). Physiologically, the overexpression strains showed elevated activities of antioxidant enzymes (CAT and SOD), higher trehalose content, and thickened conidial cell walls. Transcriptomic analysis revealed that VvbHLH primarily regulated genes involved in cell wall organization, while VvCBF activated genes related to melanin biosynthesis and oxidoreductase activity. Yeast one-hybrid assays further confirmed that VvbHLH directly bound the VvCBF promoter in V. volvacea, indicating a regulatory hierarchy between these factors. This study shows that heterologous expression of macrofungal transcription factors enhances stress tolerance and insecticidal efficacy in B. bassiana through integrated physiological and transcriptional mechanisms, offering valuable genetic resources for developing robust fungal biopesticides.
